--- !u!114 &2552889748091279201
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1930536072102207978}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9541d86e2fd84c1d9990edf0852d74ab, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Performance can be a tricky thing to improve, so solid measurement measurements
    are important. Measurable numbers allow for direct A-B comparisons. Here are
    some basics.


    <b>FPS and GPU load</b>

    The most direct way to measure
    performance is the FPS or frames per second. In VRChat, this can be seen in the
    small settings menu at the bottom. Keep in mind that the menu also causes quite
    a bit of performance overhead, so external tools are also useful.

    Usually,
    the framerate is capped. However, it is also possible to set the framerate to
    uncapped, giving an indication on how many frames could be rendered.

    In
    addition the task manager or other external tools give an indication of the current
    GPU load.


    <b>Triangles, batches and other metrics</b>

    The debug menu
    (openable at the bottom of the small setting menu in VRChat) features additional
    performance metrics. Keep in mind that as mentioned before, the VRChat menu also
    adds some overhead.


    <b>Debugging in Unity</b>

    [TBA]


    <b>External
    tools like RenderDocs</b>

    Tools like Render docs allow to dissect the frame
    step by step to check what happens exactly. While the setup overhead can be scarry,
    it is one of the most advanced tools to analyze performance.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
