--- !u!114 &8175365995031491928
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 3963187879368789397}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9541d86e2fd84c1d9990edf0852d74ab, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: '<b>A percieved perofrmance problem:</b>

    The vertex/triangle count
    of a mesh is often the first thing people will look at when looking at performance.
    However, most shaders use relatively simple vertex shaders, that just apply a
    simple matrix transformation and pass the data on. So unless you are dealing
    with nanite-level triangle counts, other factors matter way more.

    However,
    meshes can also take up quite a bit of storage space, so shaders can help offset
    the disadvantages of lower poly models.



    In addtion to that, the GPU renders
    pixels in groups of 4, called quads. If a triangle is smaller than a pixel, the
    fragment shader still needs to run on all 4 of them. This is called Quad Overdraw.
    Also, if the trianlges are close to the pixel size, aliasing can occur.



    <b>Recommended
    values for on screen triangles:</b>

    [TBA]



    <b>How to improve performance:</b>

    -
    Optimize the meshes.

    - Use different LOD levels.

    - Use pre-rendered
    impostors at a distance.

    - Use a low poly model and a tesselation shader.
    Useful for things like 3D terrains. Can also cause other performance problems
    though.

'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
